          "text": "The pattern indexes on a primitive monoclinic cell, with the diagnostic assignments:\n\n- 19.09\u00b0: 101\n- 21.19\u00b0: overlapping 200/011\n- 28.09\u00b0: 020\n- 31.86\u00b0: 002\n- 32.06\u00b0: 300\n- 32.38\u00b0: 021\n- 43.14\u00b0: overlapping 320/400/022\n- 54.75\u00b0: overlapping 500/322/402\n- 67.04\u00b0: 600\n\nThe resulting cell is approximately \\(a=8.369\\), \\(b=6.348\\), \\(c=5.613\\) \u00c5, \\(\\beta=90.44^\\circ\\), \\(V=298.2\\) \u00c5\u00b3, with \\(Z=4\\). The systematic absence of odd 0k0 reflections supports \\(P2_1/m\\) (No. 11). The structure is an ordered alkali-metal oxocuprate(I), containing approximately linear O\u2013Cu\u2013O units.\n\nThe lattice and symmetry assignment are considerably more secure than the light-atom coordinates; the latter should be regarded as a chemically constrained powder model requiring final Rietveld refinement.\n\n```cif\ndata_KNa2CuO2_powder_model\n_audit_creation_method\n;Indexed and chemically constrained from the supplied Cu-Kalpha1\npowder diffraction pattern. Atomic coordinates are a starting\nmodel for final Rietveld refinement.\n;\n\n_chemical_name_common                 'potassium disodium dioxocuprate(I)'\n_chemical_formula_sum                 'K Na2 Cu O2'\n_chemical_formula_structural          'KNa2CuO2'\n_chemical_formula_weight              180.622\n\n_cell_length_a                        8.3690\n_cell_length_b                        6.3480\n_cell_length_c                        5.6130\n_cell_angle_alpha                     90\n_cell_angle_beta                      90.44\n_cell_angle_gamma                     90\n_cell_volume                          298.20\n_cell_formula_units_Z                 4\n\n_exptl_crystal_density_diffrn         4.02\n_diffrn_radiation_type                'Cu Kalpha1'\n_diffrn_radiation_wavelength          1.5406\n\n_space_group_crystal_system           monoclinic\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt             'P 1 21/m 1'\n_space_group_name_Hall                '-P 2yb'\n_space_group_IT_number                11\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1  'x,y,z'\n2  '-x,y+1/2,-z'\n3  '-x,-y,-z'\n4  'x,-y+1/2,z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\n_atom_site_U_iso_or_equiv\nK1   K   4 f  0.5500  0.1000  0.1500  1.0  0.025\nNa1  Na  4 f  0.3500  0.0000  0.6500  1.0  0.022\nNa2  Na  4 f  0.3800  0.0200  0.9500  1.0  0.022\nCu1  Cu  4 f  0.1667  0.0500  0.2500  1.0  0.012\nO1   O   4 f  0.1872  0.2450  0.4860  1.0  0.018\nO2   O   4 f  0.1462  0.8550  0.0140  1.0  0.018\n\nloop_\n_geom_bond_atom_site_label_1\n_geom_bond_atom_site_label_2\n_geom_bond_distance\nCu1 O1 1.82\nCu1 O2 1.82\n```\n\nThe principal lower-symmetry alternative is \\(P2_1\\), but the powder pattern does not provide compelling evidence for loss of the mirror/inversion symmetry; \\(P2_1/m\\) is therefore the preferred model.",
